 ## Overview
-DAVIGS is a plug-and-play appearance modeling method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that mitigates appearance variation-induced floaters and color distortions by transforming rendered images at the image level using 3D consistent features [S1]. Unlike coupled methods that manipulate Gaussian properties (slowing rendering) or decoupled methods that only account for mild global variations, DAVIGS decouples global view-dependent appearance variations (e.g., daylight, ISPs) from local spatial changes (e.g., street lamps) by applying an affine transformation to the final rendered image [S1]. This approach ensures 3D consistency across views while retaining the low-cost advantage of decoupled modeling, as the appearance module can be discarded after optimization without impacting rendering speed [S1].
+DAVIGS is a plug-and-play appearance modeling method for Gaussian Splatting that mitigates appearance variation-induced floaters and color distortions by transforming rendered images at the image level using 3D consistent features, without compromising rendering speed [S1]. Unlike coupled methods that manipulate Gaussian properties (slowing rendering) or decoupled methods that only account for mild global variations, DAVIGS decouples global view-dependent appearance variations (e.g., daylight, ISPs) from local spatial changes (e.g., street lamps) by applying a learned affine transformation to the final rendered image [S1]. This approach ensures 3D consistency across views while allowing the appearance module to be discarded after optimization, retaining the real-time rendering performance of the base 3DGS method [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs**
+DAVIGS operates as a post-processing step on the differentiable rasterization output of a Gaussian Splatting scene. It learns an affine transformation for each pixel based on its 3D position and the view's global appearance, stabilizing optimization via identity regularization.
+
