 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Optimization time is reduced by using cell-based query instead of per-pixel query [S1]. Rendering speed is not compromised as the appearance module is discarded after optimization [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Minimal memory overhead due to the lightweight MLP and sparse hash grids [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Plug-and-play, decouples global and local appearance variations, ensures 3D consistency, fast optimization, no rendering speed penalty [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires differentiable rasterization with depth output [S1]. Performance on scenes with extreme local changes may depend on hash grid resolution [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Scenes with global appearance variations (daylight, ISPs) and local changes (street lamps) where floaters and color distortions are present [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes with no appearance variations where standard 3DGS is sufficient, or when real-time training speed is critical and cell-based query is still too slow (though this is rare) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The cell-based query reduces optimization time by computing transformation matrices for cells rather than every pixel. The rendering speed is not compromised as the module is discarded after optimization [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Adds memory overhead for hash grids, MLP weights, and global appearance embeddings. The overhead is minimal compared to coupled methods [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Plug-and-play, decouples global and local appearance variations, ensures 3D consistency, maintains real-time rendering speed after optimization [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires differentiable depth rendering. Performance on scenes with extreme local appearance changes may depend on the capacity of the hash grids and MLP [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Scenes with significant appearance variations (e.g., daylight changes, ISPs, local light sources) where standard 3DGS produces floaters or color distortions [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes with stable appearance where standard 3DGS performs well, to avoid unnecessary complexity [S1].
 
 ## Background
-DAVIGS (Decoupling Appearance Variations In GS) was proposed by Dahmani et al. in 2024 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of existing Gaussian Splatting appearance modeling methods, which are either tightly coupled with the rendering process (hindering real-time rendering) or only account for mild global variations (performing poorly in scenes with local light changes) [S1]. The method builds on the principles of 3D Gaussian Splatting and incorporates ideas from hash-based NeRF representations [S1].
